Interfaces
Interfaces are service requirement specification (SRS) , it is used to standardise the code. They help in achieving standardisation of code.

Rules
1. One or multiple class can implement from an interface by using the keyword implements. 
2.If a class implements a interface the class must provide body for all the abstract methods of the interface. 
3.By default the methods of an interface are public as well as abstract (whether the programmer explicitly mentioned it or not. 
4. A single interface can be implemented by multiple classes while implementing the methods the child classes must ensure that the same method signature is
followed as present in the parent interface. However different classes may implement the methods in their own ways(method body should be different). 
5.Interface type reference can be used to refer to implementing class however this parent type reference can refer to only the overridden method in the 
implementing class.A parent type reference cannot refer to the specialised method in the child class. However if we want to access specialised method of the 
implementing classes by using a parent interface type reference , we must perform downcasting.
6. An interface can be empty(marker or tagged interface). 
7.A single class can implement multiple interface. Multiple inheritance with respective class is not permitted in java . However we can achieve multiple 
inheritance in java indirectly using interfaces.
8.Interface cannot implement another interface but it can extend another interface.
9.By default the variables declared inside the interface are public, final and static also the methods are public and abstract. 
10.If a class has to extend to another class as well as implement from an interface , the class must extend first and then implement. 
11.If a class only partially implement an interface , a class itself is declared as abstract. 
12.We cannot create an object of an interface or we cannot instantiate an interface, however we can create a reference of an interface. This is done so 
that by using a parent interface type reference we are able to access child class object and hence polymorphism is achieved.  

