 Different scenarios for exception

In Java when exception occurs and object of that exception is generated the control along with the exception object is thrown out of the program and 
handed to RunTime System. The RTS goes back to the method which threw the exception and look for a user defined exception handler. As shown in the above 
program if the programmer has not provided any user defined exception handler,the RTS will handover the exception to the default exception handler. 
The advantage of default exception handler is that the exception object does not reach the OS and hence the system crash is prevented. However the major
drawback of DEH is that it is not able to avoid abrupt termination of the program. To overcome this problem the programmer must define UDEH. 


As seen in the above program regardless of the type exception we receive the same message this is because we are using a single catch block.
